00:00:00.625 --> 00:00:02.400
MINHAZ KAZI: Welcome
to DataVis DevTalk.

00:00:02.400 --> 00:00:05.100
I'm your host, Minhaz
Kazi, Developer Advocate

00:00:05.100 --> 00:00:06.630
for Data Studio.

00:00:06.630 --> 00:00:10.102
In this video, we will show you
how Community Connectors work.

00:00:10.102 --> 00:00:13.406
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:15.300 --> 00:00:18.270
Community Connectors are custom
connectors built in Apps Script

00:00:18.270 --> 00:00:21.120
that you can use to
connect to your own data.

00:00:21.120 --> 00:00:22.980
To build a Community
Connector, you'll

00:00:22.980 --> 00:00:26.490
develop your code in Apps Script
and then deploy the connector.

00:00:26.490 --> 00:00:29.280
Then users can start using it.

00:00:29.280 --> 00:00:32.570
Based on different user
actions in Data Studio,

00:00:32.570 --> 00:00:34.680
the Data Studio will
execute the script

00:00:34.680 --> 00:00:36.870
and invoke different functions.

00:00:36.870 --> 00:00:39.600
Then based on the
function response,

00:00:39.600 --> 00:00:41.430
Data Studio will take
different actions

00:00:41.430 --> 00:00:44.920
and render the interface as
well as the data on the screen.

00:00:44.920 --> 00:00:48.000
In this video, we will show the
Community Connector flow step

00:00:48.000 --> 00:00:48.720
by step.

00:00:48.720 --> 00:00:50.790
We will also show
the expected outcomes

00:00:50.790 --> 00:00:52.860
on screen and the
user interactions

00:00:52.860 --> 00:00:56.940
to help you understand
how to develop your code.

00:00:56.940 --> 00:00:59.430
When you develop your connector,
you will write the code

00:00:59.430 --> 00:01:01.920
and also provide certain
metadata about your connector

00:01:01.920 --> 00:01:03.580
in the manifest.

00:01:03.580 --> 00:01:06.740
The manifest will be a
file named appsscript.json,

00:01:06.740 --> 00:01:09.520
and it will be a part of
your Apps Script project.

00:01:09.520 --> 00:01:13.290
We will discuss the manifest
in details in a later video.

00:01:13.290 --> 00:01:14.940
Your code can be
in a single file

00:01:14.940 --> 00:01:18.270
or distributed across
multiple files in Apps Script.

00:01:18.270 --> 00:01:20.110
For a basic Community
Connector, you

00:01:20.110 --> 00:01:23.140
will need to define four
functions in your code.

00:01:23.140 --> 00:01:25.050
The first function
is getAuthType

00:01:25.050 --> 00:01:27.030
that tells Data Studio
the authorization

00:01:27.030 --> 00:01:28.970
type for the connector.

00:01:28.970 --> 00:01:31.260
The getConfig function
tells Data Studio

00:01:31.260 --> 00:01:34.740
how the user configuration
screen should be rendered.

00:01:34.740 --> 00:01:36.520
Then the getSchema
function provides

00:01:36.520 --> 00:01:38.530
the schema for your data.

00:01:38.530 --> 00:01:41.490
Finally, the getData function
provides the actual data

00:01:41.490 --> 00:01:44.179
for the requested
fields to Data Studio.

00:01:44.179 --> 00:01:45.720
Now let's see how
a typical connector

00:01:45.720 --> 00:01:47.210
workflow would look like.

00:01:47.210 --> 00:01:49.050
This is more of a
simplistic example

00:01:49.050 --> 00:01:52.450
to better illustrate how the
connector workflow works.

00:01:52.450 --> 00:01:55.300
Depending on your
implementation and requirements,

00:01:55.300 --> 00:01:57.600
this workflow might
somewhat vary.

00:01:57.600 --> 00:01:59.430
To use a connector,
the user first

00:01:59.430 --> 00:02:01.530
selects it in Data Studio.

00:02:01.530 --> 00:02:04.380
In our example, we are showing
the Google Fit connector

00:02:04.380 --> 00:02:06.840
that lets you fetch
your Google Fit data.

00:02:06.840 --> 00:02:08.940
Once the user has
selected the connector,

00:02:08.940 --> 00:02:11.610
Data Studio now executes
the connector script

00:02:11.610 --> 00:02:14.280
and calls the
getAuthType function.

00:02:14.280 --> 00:02:16.770
Based on the response
provided by the connector,

00:02:16.770 --> 00:02:19.320
Data Studio will then trigger
the required authorization

00:02:19.320 --> 00:02:20.010
flow.

00:02:20.010 --> 00:02:22.200
The user will see the
AuthFlow on the screen

00:02:22.200 --> 00:02:25.590
and complete it to provide
authorization to the connector.

00:02:25.590 --> 00:02:28.110
For our example, only
the Google Authorization

00:02:28.110 --> 00:02:29.970
is needed for the connector.

00:02:29.970 --> 00:02:31.650
For your implementation,
you might

00:02:31.650 --> 00:02:34.650
need additional authorization
from a third party site.

00:02:34.650 --> 00:02:36.600
After the authorization
is complete,

00:02:36.600 --> 00:02:38.700
Data Studio will call
the getConfig function

00:02:38.700 --> 00:02:40.140
from the script.

00:02:40.140 --> 00:02:44.040
In this function you can define
what configuration information

00:02:44.040 --> 00:02:46.500
you'd like to collect
from the user.

00:02:46.500 --> 00:02:48.540
Based on the response
from the script,

00:02:48.540 --> 00:02:51.240
Data Studio will render the
configuration's interface

00:02:51.240 --> 00:02:52.890
for the connector.

00:02:52.890 --> 00:02:54.480
Here we can see
that the Google Fit

00:02:54.480 --> 00:02:56.250
connector needs only
one configuration

00:02:56.250 --> 00:02:57.880
input from the user.

00:02:57.880 --> 00:03:01.350
It asks the user which type
of data it should return.

00:03:01.350 --> 00:03:03.990
The user completes all the
necessary configuration

00:03:03.990 --> 00:03:06.330
and clicks Connect.

00:03:06.330 --> 00:03:09.780
At this point Data Studio has
the necessary configuration

00:03:09.780 --> 00:03:14.020
and calls the getSchema function
to get the schema of the data.

00:03:14.020 --> 00:03:16.290
It will render the field
screen and let the user

00:03:16.290 --> 00:03:18.630
update the schema if needed.

00:03:18.630 --> 00:03:22.260
In our example, we can see that
for the selected configuration,

00:03:22.260 --> 00:03:24.540
the Google Fit connector
has a schema with five

00:03:24.540 --> 00:03:25.950
different fields.

00:03:25.950 --> 00:03:27.980
The user clicks the
Create Report button

00:03:27.980 --> 00:03:30.760
to move on to the next step.

00:03:30.760 --> 00:03:34.170
Now the user will be shown a
blank Data Studio dashboard

00:03:34.170 --> 00:03:35.850
where they can add
charts and tables

00:03:35.850 --> 00:03:38.010
to create their own report.

00:03:38.010 --> 00:03:40.410
When the user adds a
new chart Data Studio

00:03:40.410 --> 00:03:43.214
will make the getData call
to the connector script.

00:03:43.214 --> 00:03:45.630
The connector will then fetch
the data from the Google Fit

00:03:45.630 --> 00:03:48.690
API and send it
back to Data Studio.

00:03:48.690 --> 00:03:52.180
Then, Data Studio will render
the chart with the data.

00:03:52.180 --> 00:03:55.260
This is basically how a
Community Connector works.

00:03:55.260 --> 00:03:58.470
Our example showed a full
workflow from authorization

00:03:58.470 --> 00:04:00.330
to rendering the data.

00:04:00.330 --> 00:04:03.120
Now if I shared the Google Fit
dashboard with someone else

00:04:03.120 --> 00:04:05.340
and they view the
dashboard, Data Studio

00:04:05.340 --> 00:04:08.370
will only make the data
calls to the connector

00:04:08.370 --> 00:04:10.540
to refresh and update the data.

00:04:10.540 --> 00:04:13.170
So when a dashboard
is viewed only

00:04:13.170 --> 00:04:16.500
the getData calls
will get executed.

00:04:16.500 --> 00:04:19.050
So that was an overview of
how Community Connectors work

00:04:19.050 --> 00:04:20.310
in Data Studio.

00:04:20.310 --> 00:04:23.520
In our next videos we will
show you step by step how you

00:04:23.520 --> 00:04:25.380
can build your own connector.

00:04:25.380 --> 00:04:27.240
You can always go to
our developer site

00:04:27.240 --> 00:04:30.870
at
developers.google.com/DataStudio

00:04:30.870 --> 00:04:33.000
for more information.

